"\n\n<p>The judgment at the end of the previous essay must now be developed in full. The years from 1825 to 1837 are most easily narrated as a progressive story: property qualifications for voting receded, more men gained the franchise, America moved steadily toward more democracy. But that narrative omits what was happening simultaneously. While white male suffrage expanded substantially, multiple southeastern indigenous nations were forcibly expelled from their homelands at massive scale, and in several states, free Black men's voting rights were further restricted or abolished. This is not a story in which democratization came first and exclusion followed. The expansion of democratic inclusion and the hardening of exclusionary boundaries happened at the same time, by the same people, as two faces of one act.</p>\n\n<p>Three simultaneously rising lines run through this period. First, inclusion: more white men entered the electorate, candidates and parties had to compete directly for their loyalty, and political integration could no longer be primarily achieved through small-circle elite deliberation. Second, expulsion: multiple southeastern indigenous peoples were stripped of the conditions necessary for continuing to exist as political communities on their ancestral lands, pushed across the Mississippi through a combination of treaties, coercion, fraud, violence, and administrative execution. Third, the expansion of presidential power: Jackson simultaneously positioned himself as the tribune of the people against Congress, the Bank, and various elites, while converting the veto from a rarely-used constitutionality check into a regularly deployed policy weapon.</p>\n\n<p>This simultaneity was not historical coincidence. A political community defines itself by its boundaries, and the other name for a boundary is exclusion. When a community decides to include more people, it simultaneously clarifies who remains outside. More deeply, the sense of equality among those newly included often rests on contrast with those excluded. The new equality among white men — an equality no longer graded by property — was built against the background of a harder racial line. Property thresholds were dismantled; racial thresholds were reinforced. The expansion of one kind of equality and the intensification of one kind of exclusion were not coincidental events — they were structurally connected, the former partially depending on the latter. That is why this essay treats inclusion and expulsion as two faces of the same action, not two parallel topics.</p>\n\n<h3>1. A President Born Contested</h3>\n\n<p>The era opened with a contested election. The election of 1824 was the paradigmatic case of old rules failing before new rules had stabilized. Four major candidates — Jackson, John Quincy Adams, William Crawford, and Henry Clay — all came from the same fracturing political tradition. Jackson received 99 electoral votes, Adams 84, Crawford 41, Clay 37. With no candidate reaching the majority of 131, the decision went to the House under the Twelfth Amendment, choosing from the top three.</p>\n\n<p>The election's institutional importance was not just that it triggered a contingent House election, but that it occurred precisely as modes of choosing electors were rapidly changing. By 1824, eighteen states used popular voting to select presidential electors; only six still left the choice to state legislatures. In those eighteen states, Jackson received roughly 153,000 popular votes against Adams's roughly 114,000. Caution is required: no nationally standardized popular vote count existed in 1824, and treating the result as a modern popular-vote-won, presidency-lost scenario overstates the analogy.</p>\n\n<p>On February 9, 1825, the House voted by state delegation — one state, one vote. Adams won thirteen state delegations on the first ballot, Jackson seven, Crawford four. Adams then appointed Henry Clay as Secretary of State. Clay, the House Speaker, had wielded enormous influence over the House balloting despite being ineligible for the House to select. The appointment immediately detonated the Corrupt Bargain charge. Jackson's supporters called Clay the Judas of the West and treated the election as stolen. Modern scholarship generally holds that no legally definable backroom deal can be proven, but the Corrupt Bargain narrative was politically enormously effective — it became the central resource for the Jacksonian coalition's mobilization and party reconstruction through 1828.</p>\n\n<p>Adams thus carried a legitimacy deficit from his first day. Modern characterizations tend toward the same summary: he was a man of formidable credentials who was a poor politician exactly when politics was coming to matter in entirely new ways. His failure was less about narrow vision than about being unable and unwilling to fight with the tools that were displacing the old ones. He proposed a boldly nationalist program — federally funded roads, canals, a national university, scientific expeditions, observatories — but faced an environment already shifting toward mass politics and organized partisan competition. He was the person this construct's rule-change left behind.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Election of 1828 and the Party Reorganization</h3>\n\n<p>The election of 1828 converted the resentment of 1824 into institutional reorganization. Jackson won 178 electoral votes against Adams's 83, and roughly 640,000 popular votes against Adams's 500,000. It was also one of the most famously vicious early American campaigns. Jackson's marriage, his history of dueling, and his temperament were all attacked; assaults on his wife Rachel — depicting her as a bigamist and adulteress because of the timing of her divorce from a previous marriage — were particularly savage. Jackson later attributed Rachel's death in December 1828, shortly before his inauguration, partly to the campaign attacks she endured. Adams's side ran equally aggressive attacks on Jackson's conduct and character. The election of 1828 was not evidence of the construct calmly managing disagreement; it was evidence that the construct survived after disagreement was inflamed to extraordinary intensity.</p>\n\n<p>Jackson's victory generated a rapid transformation of the presidential image. He was the first president who could credibly be called self-made, the first from the West. He quickly repositioned the presidency from chief administrative manager to direct representative of the people, claiming a mandate from popular sovereignty that put him in a position to both cooperate with and confront Congress. The inauguration crowds — supporters flooding Washington, boisterously occupying the White House reception — were described as disorder by conservative critics and as the government opening to the people by supporters. The image, however exaggerated, captured something real about how Jackson was redefining what the office meant.</p>\n\n<h3>3. White Male Democracy and Its Racial Boundaries</h3>\n\n<p>Measured only by participation rates and formal franchise, the Jacksonian era was a genuine moment of democratic expansion. After the War of 1812, states progressively abolished property requirements for voting in state elections and for choosing presidential electors. The claim that men without property should nonetheless have the vote became a politically powerful demand in this period.</p>\n\n<p>But this expansion requires sharp qualification. Its direct beneficiaries were primarily white men. Revisionist scholarship over recent decades has emphasized this insistently: the expansion of white voting and the disfranchisement of Black voters were two faces of the same reconstruction of the electorate. In the decades before the Civil War, white male suffrage expanded broadly while racially defined voting qualifications were written increasingly explicitly into state law and state constitutions. Research indicates that by 1840, roughly ninety-three percent of the small free Black population had been stripped of the franchise. Pennsylvania, North Carolina, and other states specifically tightened restrictions on free Black voting in the 1830s. These materials correct a too-smooth old narrative that treats Jacksonian democracy as straightforwardly democratic progress. The more accurate formulation is that the Jacksonian era advanced mass politics for white men while intensifying the racial definition of democratic boundaries.</p>\n\n<p>This expansion of the electorate also transformed party organization. The 1824 election had exposed the failure of the congressional caucus nomination system under mass politics conditions. By 1828, the national mobilization network built around Jackson and Martin Van Buren was already far better adapted to the new environment than Adams's more gentry-style coalition. Parties in this period were becoming genuine voter-mobilization machines — more disciplined, with broader local press networks, and with the capacity to integrate voters through appointments, publicity, and symbols. The machinery for containing the remainder of partisan strife was being built more elaborately than ever before.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Institutionalizing Removal</h3>\n\n<p>As democratic inclusion expanded on one side, expulsion was being institutionalized on the other. The Indian Removal Act of 1830 was the most consequential and most serious legislation of the period. Its formal title described the exchange of lands with Indians residing in any of the states or territories, and their removal to the west of the river Mississippi. The act authorized the president to designate land west of the Mississippi for exchange, used language throughout about tribes choosing to exchange and remove, nominally preserved the language of choice, and authorized the president to fund removal, resettlement, and a year of subsistence support. But from the actual implementation, removal proceeded through treaty, coercion, bribery, threat, and in many cases violation of existing treaties and judicial rulings. The legal language of choice did not eliminate the practical coercive character.</p>\n\n<p>The bill's congressional passage was extremely close: twenty-eight to nineteen in the Senate, one hundred two to ninety-seven in the House. Jackson signed it on May 28, 1830. Those margins themselves show that even at the time, this was not a policy of uncontested consensus. A network of missionaries and humanitarian advocates, Congressman Jeremiah Evarts, and congressional opponents including Frelinghuysen, Henry Clay, and Davy Crockett publicly opposed it.</p>\n\n<p>The scale of removal is best described by specifying the measurement window rather than forcing a single number. During Jackson's presidency specifically, the government signed roughly seventy removal treaties, leading to approximately fifty thousand eastern Indians being relocated to Indian Territory. Over the full removal era of 1830–1850, roughly one hundred thousand people were pushed west of the Mississippi through treaty, fraud, intimidation, and force. The Five Civilized Tribes were the Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw, and Seminole. For the Cherokee specifically, approximately sixteen thousand were relocated; death estimates range from roughly two thousand to four thousand in the more conservative scholarly range, with four thousand being the figure most commonly cited by institutional sources and Cherokee oral tradition, and some Cherokee sources placing the figure higher.</p>\n\n<p>Whatever the precise numbers, the fact is heavy enough at any count. This was a state-organized expulsion program wrapped in legal form. Nations were forced from lands they had inhabited for generations; thousands died in transit; treaties were violated; and all of this happened in the same era, by the same political actors, who claimed to be expanding democratic governance.</p>\n\n<p>The judicial thread illuminates the nature of the failure most sharply. In Worcester v. Georgia in 1832, Chief Justice Marshall, writing for the Supreme Court, held that the Cherokee constituted a distinct political community occupying their own territory in which Georgia's laws had no force. This was an unambiguous judicial victory for the Cherokee. But Georgia refused to comply; the governor publicly defied the ruling; and Jackson took no action to compel Georgia's obedience. The famous attributed remark — \"John Marshall has made his decision; now let him enforce it\" — is of highly questionable authenticity. What Jackson actually wrote privately was that he regarded the ruling as still-born. But the practical effect is documented: federal action to enforce the ruling was not forthcoming, and removal proceeded.</p>\n\n<p>Placing this alongside the earlier essays reveals a troubling limit of this construct. The third essay traced how Marbury v. Madison placed the president himself inside the law's cage — a successful moment of the construct using its own generated checks to constrain executive power. But in the Cherokee case, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the indigenous claimants and the ruling had no effect. When the president chose not to enforce, the legislature was unwilling to compel, and white public opinion stood on the side of expulsion, a judicial victory on paper left the most vulnerable parties without protection. This construct can put the struggle inside the rule's cage; it cannot guarantee that the rules inside the cage will always stand on the side of the weakest. For those already pushed to the boundary of the political community, the failure of the check can be fatal.</p>\n\n<h3>5. The President as Tribune of the People</h3>\n\n<p>The other core of the Jacksonian era was the first systematic high-intensity redefinition of presidential power — the peak, within this series, of why the position designed to be checked keeps expanding.</p>\n\n<p>The most direct indicator was veto frequency. Jackson vetoed twelve bills in his two terms; all previous presidents together had issued ten vetoes. Adams had issued none; Monroe one; Madison seven; Washington two. But the change was not only quantitative — it was qualitative. Before Jackson, presidential vetoes rested primarily on constitutionality grounds: the president was saying that he believed the bill was unconstitutional. Jackson's 1832 veto of the Bank recharter bill was grounded simultaneously in political, economic, moral, and constitutional reasoning. The president was no longer merely saying a bill was unconstitutional; he was publicly saying that a bill was unjust, imprudent, harmful to ordinary people, and favorable to privilege — and therefore he would veto it even though Congress passed it. This was the historical conversion of the veto from a constitutional tool to a policy weapon.</p>\n\n<p>The Bank Veto Message of July 10, 1832, was the canonical text of this transformation. Jackson acknowledged the Bank's convenience to the government and usefulness to the people in many respects, then asserted that parts of its powers and privileges violated the Constitution, subverted state rights, and endangered the liberties of the people. He described the Bank as a government-favored monopoly with exclusive market privileges, argued that recharter would deliver millions more to existing stockholders including substantial foreign holders, and positioned the Bank explicitly as representing the richest class against ordinary people. This argument far exceeded the domain of legal technicality — it placed the president directly in the position of the people against privilege.</p>\n\n<p>What Jackson built was a new presidential rhetoric and practice. The president would no longer passively await constitutionally acceptable legislation from Congress but would assert — as the direct representative of the entire nation — a claim to judge the direction of policy itself. He converted the executive from chief administrative manager to popular tribune, reversing the long-standing executive deference to legislative primacy. The new fuel he found was more powerful than precedent or gravitational pull from institutional weight, and harder to discipline. By claiming to represent the whole people against a Congress of local representatives, the president had acquired a discursive leverage point above the legislature. Once that leverage point was built, it was never dismantled. Every subsequent president who sought to expand executive authority would return to it, claiming to represent the people's general will against institutions serving partial interests.</p>\n\n<h3>6. The Bank War and the Nullification Crisis</h3>\n\n<p>The clearest way to see why Jacksonianism resists ideological categorization is to place the Bank War and the Nullification Crisis side by side. Clay and Bank President Nicholas Biddle triggered the Bank War by pushing for recharter in 1832 rather than 1836, hoping to force Jackson to either sign and alienate anti-Bank voters or veto and alienate business and moderate interests. Jackson vetoed, packaged the veto as striking at privilege and corruption, and won re-election. In the second phase, federal deposits were withdrawn from the Bank and redistributed to state banks — the so-called pet banks. By late 1833 most deposits had been moved; Biddle contracted credit sharply in response, attempting to force economic pain sufficient to make Jackson relent. The contraction succeeded in generating a brief panic but not in reversing Jackson's policy. The Senate formally censured Jackson in 1834 for claiming executive authority over the Treasury that the censure resolution said belonged to Congress.</p>\n\n<p>Two interpretations of the Bank War's consequences are well established and should be placed alongside each other. The first is the anti-privilege populist reading: Jackson demolished an institution combining semi-official and private privilege in ways deeply entangled with political-business networks, serving the financial aristocracy at the expense of ordinary white voters. The second is the institutional-stability financial reading: whatever the Bank's political problems, it partially performed the functions of a modern central bank, and its destruction left the United States with a more fragmented, state-bank-dependent financial system that contributed to subsequent panics and instability.</p>\n\n<p>The Nullification Crisis showed the other face — Jackson as strong-Union federalist — making it impossible to reduce Jacksonianism to any single ideological line. South Carolina, objecting to the tariffs of 1828 and 1832, claimed the right to declare federal tariff law void within its borders. Jackson's Nullification Proclamation of December 10, 1832, systematically dismantled the constitutional basis of state nullification. He declared the South Carolina ordinance subversive of the Constitution and warned that its logic, if carried out, would quickly leave the United States without national existence. He denied that states possessed the right to nullify or secede. The crisis was defused through two simultaneous measures: the Force Bill, authorizing the president to use military force to ensure collection of the tariff, and Clay's Compromise Tariff, gradually reducing rates. The same president denounced as King Andrew for his assault on the Bank was simultaneously asserting federal military supremacy over state resistance to federal law.</p>\n\n<p>Jacksonianism's consistent thread was not small government and not state rights. It was making the president the pivot of national direction and execution — deploying anti-privilege language in the Bank context and strong-union language in the tariff context, but in both cases positioning the presidency as the nation's ultimate authority. Understanding this prevents being confused by the apparently contradictory positions.</p>\n\n<h3>7. The Second Party System and King Andrew</h3>\n\n<p>Jackson's deepest institutional legacy was a new party system. His supporters coalesced into what became the modern Democratic Party's predecessor; his opponents, through the mid-1830s, organized as the Whig Party. In eight years, Jackson converted a loosely personal coalition into a disciplined, nationally organized party machine — and his controversial methods simultaneously forced into existence an organized opposition.</p>\n\n<p>The Whigs emerged not only from policy disagreement but from structural recoil against the style of presidential rule. The Bank War, the removal of deposits, the normalization of the veto, partisan control of executive appointments, and Jackson's personal political intensity led opponents to portray him as King Andrew — a president using popular mandate to burst through constitutional constraints. For the Whigs, the problem was not just any single policy but whether the presidency was consuming the congressional-centered constitutional order.</p>\n\n<p>Placed within this series' framework, the Whig Party's emergence was one more activation of the familiar rhythm. Every major expansion of executive power triggered a counterforce. Jefferson's expansion ran into Marshall's court. Jackson's elevation of the presidency to unprecedented heights immediately generated a party dedicated to constraining it. The Whigs were not an accident — they were the construct's self-correction in response to Jacksonian expansion. The corrective force this time did not come from another branch but from a newly organized opposition party seeking, on the electoral battlefield, to pull the elevated presidency back down.</p>\n\n<h3>8. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>Placing the twelve years of John Quincy Adams and Jackson back in this series, the most important thing to carry forward is the judgment that runs through the whole essay: inclusion and exclusion at the same time.</p>\n\n<p>This construct during this period brought more white men into the political community, built the machine for containing partisan strife more elaborately than ever before, and simultaneously expelled southeastern indigenous nations and pushed free Black men further out of the electorate in state after state. Democratic expansion was two-sided: the same gesture that brought some people in was more thoroughly pushing others out. That is the deepest remainder's form in the Jacksonian era — not first democracy and then exclusion, but the boundaries of democracy themselves being drawn through exclusion.</p>\n\n<p>At the same time, presidential power reached its first great peak — powered now by a new fuel, the direct mandate of popular sovereignty. Jackson converted the veto from a constitutional to a policy instrument; converted the president from executive manager to tribune of the people. But this power could not be fitted into any single ideological framework, because he was anti-privilege on the Bank and strongly unionist on nullification, both in service of the same goal: making the presidency the pivot of the nation. Once the leverage point of direct popular mandate was built into presidential practice, it was never dismantled. Every subsequent president seeking to expand executive authority would return to it.</p>\n\n<p>What follows is the era in which this newly formed Second Party System begins to run. A severe economic panic will immediately test the financial legacy Jackson left. And the geographic fault line first cut in Missouri, re-politicized with every new territorial acquisition, will be cut deeper and deeper along the path of annexation and expansion. That is the story from Van Buren to the eve of the Civil War.</p>\n\n      "
